 ## Overview
-Slice-by-Slice (Sheet Buffer) Splatting is a volume rendering optimization that organizes splats into cache-sheets aligned parallel to the volume face most closely parallel to the image plane, then composites these sheets slice-by-slice [S1, S3, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from ray-casting and screen-quad methods by compositing splats into aligned sheet buffers rather than sampling along rays or rendering full-screen quads, trading the "sparkling" of per-splat compositing for "popping" artifacts when the view crosses 45° relative to the data axes [S2][S3, unofficial].
